[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Node types are derived from two axes: normative or descriptive, universal or particular

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID
TITLE: Node types are derived from two axes: normative or descriptive, universal or particular
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A durable claim is found that fits no cell and is neither a decision nor a narrative, or a cell proves empty after real use.
STATEMENT: >>>
The node types of the design graph are DERIVED from two binary axes, not
enumerated by taste. Cross them and four of the six types fall out; the
remaining two sit outside the grid because they operate on it.

AXIS ONE, normative or descriptive. Does the node say how the world OUGHT to
be, or how it IS? The test is what you change on finding a counterexample
tomorrow: the world means normative, this node means descriptive. A normative
node can be VIOLATED. A descriptive node cannot be violated, only be WRONG.

AXIS TWO, universal or particular. Is it a claim about every case, or about one
concrete case?

                universal                     particular
  normative     REQUIREMENT                   USE CASE
                what must always be true      one path that must work
  descriptive   MECHANISM                     EVIDENCE
                how it always behaves         what happened this once

DECISION and NARRATIVE are outside the grid and act on it: a decision CHANGES
what the norms are, and a narrative ASSEMBLES the grid for a person to read
start to finish.

THREE CONSEQUENCES, each of which was an open question before the grid and is
answered by it rather than by argument.

A DESIGN PRINCIPLE IS A REQUIREMENT. Normative, universal. It differs from an
ordinary requirement only in having unbounded scope -- it constrains work not
yet conceived -- and that difference lives in its statement, not in machinery.
The one mechanical change it asks for is that Governed_By admit a REQUIREMENT
target, so a node can cite the principle it serves.

A USE CASE IS ITS OWN TYPE, and it was the empty cell rather than an addition.
A requirement quantifies over everything; a use case is one trace the design
must survive. That is the shape of a fitness test: describe a slice in prose,
hold the design against it, and a conflict becomes findable rather than felt.

AN IMPERATIVE STEP IS NOT IN THE GRID AT ALL. "Run exactly this command" is
neither mood -- it makes no claim about the world. It is WORK, and it dies with
the plan that held it. So variable planning granularity needs no new vocabulary:
high trust is few coarse work nodes, low trust is many fine ones, same type
either way.

WHAT THE GRID ALSO SETTLES BY OMISSION. There is no separate node for a
question, a rationale, a constraint or a note. A question is a DECISION at
STATUS open. A rationale is a field. A constraint is a REQUIREMENT. A note is
either evidence, or it is not durable.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The types were being added one at a time, each justified on its own, which is
how a vocabulary grows to a dozen entries that overlap at the edges and leave
gaps nobody notices. Two axes generate the same four types, predict the one that
was missing, and give a mechanical test for the next candidate: name its mood
and its quantifier, and it either lands in a cell or it is not a claim.

It also makes the boundary between the disposable and the durable halves
legible without a second vocabulary. Everything in the grid is a claim and
therefore outlives the work that produced it; everything imperative is work and
does not.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Arrived at 2026-08-28 while the operator was trying to decide, one at a time,
whether a design principle warranted a node type and where review feedback
belonged. Both questions dissolved once the axes were named, which is the
argument for the grid over a list.

STILL OPEN and deliberately not settled here: whether PLAN and WORK survive as
node types once beads owns execution. That is DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN's, and the
grid does not answer it -- the grid says what a CLAIM is, and work is not a
claim.

USE CASE and NARRATIVE are not in the grammar yet. Adding them is a values.nix
edit and a regenerate, not a change to this decision.
<<<
